course: biology
lesson: 79
title: Animals of Australia and New Zealand: kangaroos, koalas, wombats, kiwis
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Australia and New Zealand drifted away from the other continents long ago, and their animals went their own strange way: mammals that lay eggs, babies the size of a jellybean in pouches, and birds that walk because nothing on the ground used to hunt them.
explain: Most Australian mammals are marsupials, whose tiny babies finish growing in a pouch: the red kangaroo, the koala, the wombat and the Tasmanian devil are all pouch mammals.
explain: The puzzles are real: a wombat makes cube-shaped droppings, a platypus finds shrimps with its eyes shut by sensing electricity, and a kea parrot will happily take apart your car's windscreen wiper.
explain: Cartoons call the koala a bear, draw kangaroos as boxers that hop forever, and make every Australian animal a cuddly toy; real koalas are not bears, and a cassowary's kick or a platypus's spur is no joke.
fact: Red kangaroos are the largest marsupials, can stand taller than a man and hop at over 50 kilometres per hour in bursts; their springy tendons make hopping cheap in energy, and a newborn joey is about the size of a jellybean.
fact: Koalas are marsupials, not bears; they rest about 18 to 22 hours a day because eucalyptus leaves are tough, low in energy and poisonous to most animals, and they are not drunk on eucalyptus.
fact: Wombats are the only animals known to make cube-shaped droppings, shaped by firmer and softer parts of the gut, and they leave them on rocks and logs to mark territory; their pouch faces backwards, so digging does not fill it with soil.
fact: The platypus is an egg-laying mammal with a duck-like bill; under water it shuts its eyes, ears and nose and senses tiny electric signals from prey, and males have a venomous spur on each back leg that causes terrible pain, so wild platypuses must never be handled.
fact: The echidna is the other egg-laying mammal, with spines and a sticky tongue for ants and termites; its baby, a puggle, hatches in a pouch, and a frightened echidna digs down so only its spines show.
fact: The kiwi is a flightless, mostly nocturnal New Zealand bird with nostrils at the tip of its long beak; its egg is about 20 percent of the mother's weight, one of the biggest for body size of any bird.
fact: New Zealand had no ground-hunting mammals before people came, so birds like the kiwi and kakapo did not need to fly.
fact: The kea is a clever, curious mountain parrot from New Zealand, famous for pulling apart cars, bags and tents, and its warbling call seems to set other keas playing.
fact: The laughing kookaburra is a big kingfisher whose family groups laugh together at dawn and dusk to mark territory; it eats snakes, lizards, insects and mice.
fact: The Tasmanian devil is the largest surviving meat-eating marsupial, about the size of a small dog, with a bone-crushing bite; a face tumour disease has killed many, so disease-free groups are kept as insurance.
fact: The emu is the second tallest bird, up to about 1.9 metres, and runs at around 50 kilometres per hour; the father sits on the eggs for about 8 weeks, hardly eating, and in the 1932 Emu War the emus mostly won.
fact: The southern cassowary has a dagger-like claw up to about 12 centimetres on each foot; it avoids people, but fed cassowaries lose their fear and can kick if fed or cornered, so it must be watched from a distance and never fed.
fact: The tuatara looks like a lizard but is the last survivor of an ancient group of reptiles; it lives on cool New Zealand islands and can live over 100 years.
exercise: About how big is a newborn red kangaroo joey? | About the size of a jellybean | About the size of a small pet cat | About the size of a big football | About the size of a man's hand
exercise: Why does fast hopping cost a kangaroo surprisingly little energy? | Its stretchy tendons work like springs | It only ever hops downhill, never up | Its pouch pushes it forward at each hop | It is lighter than air
exercise: What kind of animal is a koala? | A marsupial, not a bear | A small kind of bear | A kind of monkey | A kind of sloth
exercise: Why do koalas rest about 18 to 22 hours a day? | Eucalyptus leaves give little energy and are hard to digest | Eucalyptus leaves make them drunk and too dizzy to climb about | They hunt all night and sleep all day | They are always too cold to move
exercise: What is special about wombat droppings? | They are cube-shaped | They glow in the dark | They are a bright blue | They smell of eucalyptus flowers
exercise: Why does a wombat's pouch face backwards? | So digging does not fill it with soil | So the baby can see where they have been | So rain runs into it | So the baby falls out when it is ready
exercise: How does a platypus find food under water? | It senses tiny electric signals from prey with its bill | It looks around with its big eyes held wide open under the water | It listens with its ears under water | It follows the smell with its nose
exercise: What is dangerous about a male platypus? | A venomous spur on its back legs | Sharp, pointed teeth like a shark's | A poisonous bill | Stiff, sharp spines all over its back
exercise: Which two mammals lay eggs? | The platypus and the echidna | The kangaroo and the koala | The wombat and the emu | The kiwi and the tuatara
exercise: What is a baby echidna called? | A puggle | A joey | A cub | A pup
exercise: Where are a kiwi's nostrils? | At the very tip of its long beak | On top of its head, behind the eyes | Under its tiny wings | Next to its eyes
exercise: Why did kiwis not need to fly? | New Zealand had no ground-hunting mammals before people | They are too heavy to fly, like all birds that live on the ground | They lost their wings in fights | Flying is banned in New Zealand
exercise: What is the kea famous for? | Pulling apart cars, bags and tents | Swimming deep under the sea for fish | Laughing loudly at dawn in the trees | Laying the biggest egg in the world
exercise: Why do kookaburras laugh at dawn and dusk? | Family groups call together to mark their territory | They are laughing at the other birds that wake up later than them | They are calling their food | They are warning about rain
exercise: What happened to many Tasmanian devils? | A face tumour disease killed many | They were all eaten by hungry kangaroos | They all swam across to New Zealand | They slowly turned into wild dingoes
exercise: Who sits on emu eggs? | The father, for about 8 weeks | The mother, for about 8 days only | Both parents taking turns hourly | Nobody, the warm sun hatches them
exercise: What is the tuatara? | The last survivor of an ancient group of reptiles | A young crocodile from New Zealand that has not grown up yet | A kind of iguana that came from America | A spiky frog that lives on rocks
exercise: What should you do if you see a cassowary on a rainforest track? | Watch from a distance and never feed it | Walk up slowly and offer it some fruit from your hand | Chase it off the track | Try to stroke its feathers
test: «koala bears are so cute, wanna hug one» What is right? | Koalas are marsupials, not bears, and wild ones should be left alone | Yes, koalas are the smallest kind of bear, so hugging them is safe | Yes, koalas love hugs from people | No, koalas are a kind of monkey
test: «koalas are just stoned on eucalyptus all day lol» What is right? | That's a myth; they rest because the leaves give little energy | Yes, eucalyptus makes them drunk | Yes, and that is why they keep falling out of the trees all the time | No, koalas only sleep in winter
test: «saw a platypus, gonna pick it up for a selfie» What is the best answer? | Don't: males have a venomous spur that causes terrible pain | Fine, platypuses are harmless | Fine, as long as you hold it by the bill so it cannot bite you | Fine, they love being held
test: In a cartoon, a kangaroo wears boxing gloves and hops forever without getting tired. What is real? | Springy tendons make hopping cheap, but top speed is only for short bursts | Kangaroos never get tired at any speed, so they can hop all day and night | Kangaroos cannot hop, they walk on four legs | Kangaroos fly short distances
test: «that wombat poo's square, someone's messing with us» What is right? | Wombats really make cube-shaped droppings, shaped in the gut | Someone carved it with a knife | Wombats sit on square rocks to squash it into shape on purpose | It is not poo, it is a wombat egg
test: «cassowary looks friendly, I'll give it my sandwich» Why is that a bad idea? | Fed cassowaries lose their fear of people and may kick with sharp claws | Sandwiches make cassowaries sleepy | Cassowaries only eat meat, so the bread would make them angry and attack | It is fine, cassowaries never kick
test: «crap, a kea's nicking the wiper off the car!» Why do keas do this? | They are very clever and curious and try out everything they find | They are hungry and eat rubber | They hate cars and attack them to drive them out of the mountains | Someone trained them to steal
test: Why might scientists keep a healthy group of Tasmanian devils on a separate island? | As insurance, in case the face tumour disease kills the rest | To stop them eating all the baby kangaroos on the mainland | Because devils like islands better | To breed them into dogs
test: What would happen if rats and stoats spread through a New Zealand forest? | Ground-nesting birds like kiwis would lose eggs and chicks | Kiwis would quickly learn to fly away into the trees | Nothing, kiwis are too fast | The birds would turn round and eat the rats and stoats instead
test: «emus are just Aussie ostriches» What is true? | Emus are a different bird, the second tallest after the ostrich | Yes, they are the same species, just living in a different country | Yes, ostriches are baby emus | No, emus can fly
test: «the tuatara is just a lizard, innit» What is right? | It looks like one but is the last of a separate ancient group | Yes, it is a common green iguana that escaped from a pet shop | Yes, it is a baby crocodile | No, it is a kind of frog
test: Why is a kiwi's egg remarkable? | It is about 20 percent of the mother's weight | It is the smallest egg of any bird in the world | It is square like a wombat dropping | It hatches in one day
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the koala? | It is a marsupial whose long rest saves the scant energy of eucalyptus leaves | It is a species of bear intoxicated by the leaves of the eucalyptus tree | It is a primate which rests only during the winter months | It is a nocturnal predator which hunts throughout the night
exam: Identify the means by which the platypus detects its prey beneath the water. | By sensing minute electrical signals with its bill | By sight, its eyes remaining fully open beneath the water | By hearing, its ears remaining open beneath the water | By the sense of smell, its nostrils remaining open
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the incubation of the eggs of the emu. | The male incubates the eggs for some eight weeks, scarcely eating | The female incubates the eggs for approximately eight days | The eggs are incubated by the warmth of the sun alone, being buried in sand | Both parents incubate the eggs in alternating hours
exam: State the two mammals of Australia which reproduce by laying eggs. | The platypus and the echidna | The red kangaroo and the koala | The wombat and the Tasmanian devil | The emu and the laughing kookaburra
